# Git Workflow

This project follows a professional Git workflow.

Before making any code changes:

1. Always synchronize the latest `develop` branch.

```bash
git checkout develop
git pull origin develop
```

2. Create a new branch from `develop`.

Branch naming convention:

- feature/<feature-name>
- fix/<bug-name>
- hotfix/<critical-fix>
- refactor/<module-name>
- chore/<task-name>
- docs/<documentation-name>

Examples:

```
feature/reservation-system
feature/customer-membership
feature/landing-page
feature/admin-dashboard
fix/login-validation
refactor/menu-service
docs/api-specification
```

3. Never work directly on:

- develop
- main
- master

4. Make small and meaningful commits.

One completed feature or logical change should equal one commit.

Avoid giant commits containing unrelated changes.

5. Follow Conventional Commits.

Examples:

```
feat: add reservation booking flow

feat: implement loyalty point calculation

fix: resolve navbar responsive issue

refactor: simplify reservation service

docs: update API documentation

style: improve menu card spacing

test: add reservation unit tests

chore: update dependencies
```

6. Before every commit ensure:

- Project builds successfully.
- ESLint passes.
- TypeScript has zero errors.
- Tests pass (if applicable).
- No unused imports.
- No console.log left behind.
- No TODO comments unless intentionally documented.

7. Never push directly to:

- develop
- main

Push only to your working branch.

Example:

```bash
git push -u origin feature/reservation-system
```

8. Merge into `develop` only after code review or explicit user approval.

9. Never rewrite public Git history unless explicitly instructed.

10. Keep commit history clean, readable, and professional.

Think of the Git history as permanent project documentation.